  "tail": "## Working out Fix(cf) from `thm-cofinality-basics`\n\nFrom `items/thm-cofinality-basics.md`:\n- (b): `cf(0) = 0` and `cf(α+1) = 1` for every ordinal α.\n- (c): for a limit ordinal λ, `cf(λ)` is an infinite cardinal with `cf(cf(λ)) = cf(λ)`.\n\nSo among successors, `cf(α+1)=1` is a fixed point only when `α+1=1`, i.e. α=0 — the sole successor fixed point is 1. Among limits, `cf(λ)` is always an infinite cardinal, so `cf(λ)=λ` forces λ itself to be an infinite cardinal, and then `cf(λ)=λ` is exactly the definition of **regular** given in `def-cofinality.md` (\"regular when cf(κ)=κ\"). Every infinite cardinal is a limit ordinal (`thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md` [L4]), so this is exhaustive.\n\n**Fix(cf) = {0, 1} ∪ {regular infinite cardinals}.** Singular infinite cardinals (e.g. ℵ_ω per `thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md` clause (c): `cf(ℵ_ω)=ℵ_0 < ℵ_ω`) are *not* in this set.\n\n## Checking the repaired clause against that\n\n```\n### def-cofinality — CERTIFY\nCURRENT TEXT READ: \"and it holds only at $0$, at $1$, and at infinite cardinals, where it is exactly the regularity defined above and so fails at every singular one. Calling an ordinal regular would therefore say nothing new, which is why the words are attached to cardinals here.\"\nASSESSMENT: Reading the sentence whole (not truncating at the first comma-list), it asserts: cf(α)=α holds at 0, at 1, and — among infinite cardinals — exactly where that condition is the already-defined regularity, \"and so fails at every singular one.\" That final clause is not decoration; it is the sentence's own explicit denial that the condition holds at singular infinite cardinals, so the net claim is {0, 1} ∪ {regular infinite cardinals}, matching what I derived above from thm-cofinality-basics (b),(c) independently. This is a correct restatement, not the pre-repair error (which asserted cf(α)=α at *all* infinite cardinals with no such qualifier). It is also consistent with:\n  - def-cofinality's own Definition (\"regular when cf(κ)=κ; singular when cf(κ)≠κ ... singular means exactly cf(κ)<κ\").\n  - the item's own second Remark (\"A cardinal that is reachable from below by fewer than κ steps ... That is exactly the failure of regularity\").\n  - thm-regularity-of-the-alephs.md clause (c): \"cf(ℵ_ω)=ℵ_0, and ℵ_0<ℵ_ω, so ℵ_ω is singular\" — a singular infinite cardinal that indeed does NOT satisfy cf(α)=α, exactly as the repaired clause now says.\nThe following sentence, \"Calling an ordinal regular would therefore say nothing new,\" is licensed: since cf(λ)=λ forces λ to already be a cardinal (never a \"new\" non-cardinal ordinal case beyond the trivial 0/1), attaching \"regular\" to general ordinals would just reproduce the cardinal notion plus two trivial extra points.\nOne caveat for the record, not a mathematical error: the clause is dense — a reader who stops parsing at \"...and at infinite cardinals,\" before reaching the restrictive \"where...fails at every singular one,\" could momentarily misread it as claiming ALL infinite cardinals qualify. But the qualifying clause is in the very same sentence, not a separate one, and it explicitly and unambiguously excludes singular cardinals, so the sentence taken as written is true. This is prose density, not a false claim, and falls under \"imperfection at the level of the letter.\"\nCOLLATERAL: none found. Nothing else in the item asserts or depends on the old (over-broad) claim; the third Remark's statement that cf(κ)-regularity \"is a theorem and not part of the definition\" and cites thm-cofinality-basics is unaffected and still accurate; the Definition section's exhaustive-cases claim is unaffected.\nSTAMPS: verification block (frontmatter lines 15–16) holds only `precheck: n/a`. No `judge:` block, no `audited:` stamp are present — the correct, intentional post-Alpha-removal state per this dispatch. Not a defect.\n```\n\n## Two further items, as requested\n\n**1. Fourth Remark heading — \"Only one notion of 'cofinal' exists in this library.\"** This is **overstated / false as literally written**. `items/def-subnet.md` defines: *\"there is a map φ:E→D such that... The displayed condition says that φ is **eventually cofinal**.\"* That is a second, distinct formal notion carrying the word \"cofinal\" — an index-map property between directed sets in the theory of nets, unrelated to ordinal subsets. The remark's *body* is narrowly and correctly scoped (\"`def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal` introduces cofinal subsets... neither the cofinality function nor the regular/singular vocabulary. Both are introduced here\"), which is accurate and matches `def-cofinal-subset-of-an-ordinal.md`'s own remark (\"What is not defined at this point in the reading order... they are introduced later, on Cardinal Arithmetic, Cofinality and the Alephs\"). But the *heading* drops that scope and claims uniqueness across the whole library, which is false given `def-subnet.md`. Per the dispatch, I am **not** treating this as grounds to refuse the item this round, and no adjudication in this round licenses editing it — reporting it only.\n\n**2. Verification block.** Confirmed: frontmatter carries `verification: { precheck: n/a }` only — no `judge:` block, no `audited:` stamp. This is the correct intended state (stale stamp already removed by Alpha before the repair); not a defect.\n\n1 certified, 0 refused."
